WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:06.220 --> 00:00:15.850
August 2017, Cassini captured six images of
Enceladus, one of the most intriguing

00:00:15.850 --> 00:00:17.590
moons in the solar system.

00:00:19.210 --> 00:00:23.170
Enceladus is a relatively small moon of
Saturn.

00:00:23.620 --> 00:00:28.240
That's pretty easy to ignore, but once you
pay attention to it, hosts a lot of

00:00:28.240 --> 00:00:29.620
surprises.

00:00:34.710 --> 00:00:40.830
We've known something was unusual about this
icy world ever since the Voyager mission took

00:00:40.830 --> 00:00:42.750
photographs in 1980.

00:00:44.610 --> 00:00:46.980
We thought that insolence would be frozen
solid.

00:00:47.580 --> 00:00:53.420
And yet we knew from Voyager data the
surface of Enceladus was bright white.

00:00:53.430 --> 00:00:59.880
And we could see on the surface that vast
tracts of it were smooth, at least at the

00:00:59.880 --> 00:01:01.950
resolution that we had with Voyager.

00:01:01.950 --> 00:01:07.980
And that immediately says that there's been
internal activity because that's really on an

00:01:07.980 --> 00:01:11.760
airless moon. That's the only process that
could erase craters.

00:01:13.410 --> 00:01:21.600
Scientists suspected something was actively
resurfacing Enceladus, filling in its craters

00:01:21.600 --> 00:01:23.640
to make it smooth and bright.

00:01:25.350 --> 00:01:31.920
Then Cassini sent back pictures of Enceladus
backlit and all was revealed.

00:01:32.980 --> 00:01:38.650
We saw these icy jets shooting out from
Enceladus and everyone was so amazed that a

00:01:38.650 --> 00:01:45.640
moon so tiny and assumed to be a frozen
solid ice cube could be so active.

00:01:51.170 --> 00:01:55.820
Jets have almost luminous material spraying
out of geysers.

00:01:57.140 --> 00:02:02.070
When I first saw a picture of a geyser on
Enceladus, I was floored.

00:02:02.090 --> 00:02:05.720
That's amazing. I had no idea that that was
even possible.

00:02:06.140 --> 00:02:11.270
The Cassini discovery of geysers on Enceladus
was a game changer.

00:02:11.510 --> 00:02:14.510
All of a sudden, here's water jetting out.

00:02:14.510 --> 00:02:16.760
It was like, too good to be true.

00:02:17.900 --> 00:02:22.550
Cassini revealed the geysers are blasting out
liquid water.

00:02:22.860 --> 00:02:26.720
Enceladus is not a solid ball of ice.

00:02:27.620 --> 00:02:32.840
As we got more data from Cassini, we found
that Enceladus had a wobble that was too

00:02:32.840 --> 00:02:36.440
large for a body that was frozen solid all
the way through.

00:02:36.440 --> 00:02:41.090
And that told us that a liquid water ocean
circled a rocky core.

00:02:41.540 --> 00:02:47.480
Enceladus has liquid water under its surface
and it may very well be an ocean basically

00:02:47.480 --> 00:02:49.690
covering the inside of that moon.

00:02:49.700 --> 00:02:51.680
But where's the heat coming from?

00:02:53.870 --> 00:02:58.280
When planets and moons form, their cores are
incredibly hot.

00:02:59.270 --> 00:03:02.240
But over time, they cooled down.

00:03:02.390 --> 00:03:05.600
The smaller the planet, the faster it cools.

00:03:06.820 --> 00:03:08.950
A tiny world like Enceladus.

00:03:08.980 --> 00:03:13.450
Over a billion miles from the sun should
have frozen solid by now.

00:03:15.750 --> 00:03:16.830
That's what we expected.

00:03:16.830 --> 00:03:20.550
If things are smaller, then they will be
roughly dead and they'd be covered by

00:03:20.550 --> 00:03:24.060
craters. But Enceladus shows us that that's
not the case at all.

00:03:26.230 --> 00:03:31.270
How could a tiny moon so far from the sun
have enough warmth for liquid water?

00:03:31.420 --> 00:03:34.390
One idea is tidal heating.

00:03:35.940 --> 00:03:39.030
If you've ever played racquetball, you know,
as you play the game, the ball heats up.

00:03:39.030 --> 00:03:43.620
And this is because as the ball hits the
racket or the wall, it's getting squished and

00:03:43.620 --> 00:03:44.730
then it relaxes.

00:03:47.980 --> 00:03:54.220
Saturn's gravity squishes and relaxes
Enceladus as it orbits the planet, heating it

00:03:54.220 --> 00:03:55.570
like a racquet ball.

00:03:57.630 --> 00:04:03.360
But this alone wouldn't generate enough heat
to stop and celadus water from freezing.

00:04:04.020 --> 00:04:07.230
Something else must be going on in the core.

00:04:09.470 --> 00:04:15.050
What if the core of Enceladus is actually
kind of gravelly instead of it just being

00:04:15.050 --> 00:04:18.840
solid? It's actually made of rocks and
pebbles and gravel all put together.

00:04:18.860 --> 00:04:23.630
Then what happens is as the tides are
stretching and squeezing it, those rocks are

00:04:23.630 --> 00:04:27.770
rubbing together and that actually generates
even more energy.

00:04:31.270 --> 00:04:38.650
2017. A computer model based on Cassini data
revealed this tidal friction generates more

00:04:38.650 --> 00:04:41.950
energy than America's biggest power station.

00:04:43.520 --> 00:04:49.020
Water heated to 194°F, rises to the surface.

00:04:49.040 --> 00:04:54.650
It sprays through cracks in the moon's South
pole, creating misty plumes.

00:04:58.260 --> 00:05:03.600
As we flew seven times through and tasted and
sampled the gas and the particles, we found

00:05:03.600 --> 00:05:08.820
salty particles that the ocean was salty,
very much like the Earth's ocean.

00:05:08.850 --> 00:05:10.470
We found hydrocarbons.

00:05:10.470 --> 00:05:12.510
Methane, carbon dioxide.

00:05:13.020 --> 00:05:20.640
When we found the key ingredients for life
coming out of the jets of Enceladus so

00:05:20.640 --> 00:05:21.720
remarkable.

00:05:24.840 --> 00:05:31.500
In 2018, scientists reanalyzed the Enceladus
data and found something even more

00:05:31.500 --> 00:05:37.570
remarkable. Complex organic molecules.

00:05:38.890 --> 00:05:43.720
What they found were larger organic compounds
than initially thought there.

00:05:43.750 --> 00:05:46.990
At first it was just dust and water and some
some basic organics.

00:05:47.020 --> 00:05:52.030
Now they're seeing more complex stuff coming
up from Enceladus interior.

00:05:52.030 --> 00:05:55.660
And that asks the question, what else is
down there?

00:05:56.870 --> 00:06:01.610
On Earth, we find life huddled around hot
vents on the seabed.

00:06:01.640 --> 00:06:04.490
Could the same be true in the oceans of
Enceladus?

00:06:06.520 --> 00:06:11.470
Could these complex organic molecules be
signs of life?

00:06:12.310 --> 00:06:13.780
My favorite name for them is goo.

00:06:13.990 --> 00:06:19.940
They're gooey things, and think of life as a
collection of gooey like molecules.

00:06:19.970 --> 00:06:23.530
Now, that doesn't mean that all gooey
molecules are biological, but certainly

00:06:23.530 --> 00:06:26.500
biology makes use of these complex organic
molecules.

00:06:28.810 --> 00:06:35.710
Deep in Enceladus, oceans, heat from
hydrothermal vents drives chemical reactions,

00:06:36.820 --> 00:06:43.300
combining simple molecules like methane and
hydrogen into longer, complex organic

00:06:43.300 --> 00:06:51.660
molecules. Complex molecules that could
serve as the precursors to life.

00:06:52.350 --> 00:06:54.720
What's amazing is the chemistry of that
ocean.

00:06:55.470 --> 00:06:57.210
Everything needed for life is there.

00:06:57.810 --> 00:07:00.150
The eternal question is, is there life in the
universe?

00:07:00.540 --> 00:07:03.480
And Enceladus is a great place to try and
answer that question.

00:07:09.050 --> 00:07:14.360
And Cassini revealed secrets of another moon
of Saturn with amazing chemistry.

00:07:15.320 --> 00:07:21.740
A giant moon with earth like features
rivers, lakes and dunes.

00:07:22.160 --> 00:07:23.030
Titan.

